  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nEarly afternoon GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis reveal a trough \nover Manitoba, extending south into the Dakotas with a deeper trough \nfurther upstream over the Pacific Northwest. This is supporting two \nareas of low pressure over western Canada. The southern shortwave \nmoving northeast through this evening from the Dakotas to Northern \nOntario quickly shoves a sfc low with it over western Lake Superior \nbefore it phases with the low to the northwest. The rest of this \nafternoon remains dry as warm air advection yields unseasonable \ntemps well above normal (+15-25 F) in the 60s. South winds become \nbreezy by this evening as a 50-60 kt LLJ sets up. Gusts of 20-25 mph \nare expected with gusts around 30 mph in areas of downslope flow. \nFavorable dynamics support a quick hitting round of rain showers and \nthunderstorms with the cold front pressing through. PWATs briefly \nreach 1 to 1.15 inches, supporting 0.1 to 0.5 inches of rainfall \nover the west half. Lesser amounts below 0.25 inches are expected in \nthe east, mainly around 0.1 inch as these thunderstorms weaken. Bulk \nshear will be plenty strong given the aforementioned LLJ, but MUCAPE \nvalues struggle to reach 500 J/kg and decrease into tonight. Modest \nlapse rates could support gusty winds when these arrive in the west \nshould they hold together, but arrival after sunset is not favorable \nfor strong storms to persist. SPC has a marginal risk in the west \nfor damaging winds.\n\nThe UP will be in the wake of the cold front Thursday morning as the \nlow moves to Quebec. Expect cooler temps and breezy west winds \ncourtesy of CAA and pressure rises. Gusts in the Keweenaw and near \nLake Superior will likely be between 25-35 mph. Temps peak in the \nupper 40s to upper 50s, coldest northwest. Dry air initially clears \nskies out early Thursday morning, but colder air begins to result in \nlake effect clouds. There is a low chance for lake effect rain \nshowers Thursday night in the far east (15-24% chance); any rainfall \namounts would be negligible. Temps return to near normal Thursday \nnight in the 20s to mid 30s, coldest interior west. The exception to \nthis is for mid 30s to upper 40s far east where insulating lake \neffect cloud cover resides.\n\nCooler and drier weather continues into Friday as surface high \npressure moves directly overhead, tapering off winds. Lake effect \ncloud cover decreases, yielding plenty of sunshine as 850 mb temps \nwarm. High pressure extending overhead through the weekend likely \nkeeps dry weather going (85-90% chance). Temperatures return to the \n50s with morning lows warming back to the 40s by the start of next \nweek. Guidance then hints at a stronger system moving out of the \nPlains and approaching the Great Lakes during the early part of next \nweek, but a lot of variation with respect, to track, timing, and \nintensity remains. Thus 25-50% PoPS return Monday night and persist \nthe remainder of the forecast.",
